WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.700
A newborn neuron in the developing cortex has no legs.

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.360
It is born beside the fluid-filled ventricle.

3
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.800
Its layer lies near the outer surface, across a thick wall.

4
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:20.960
Later in development, it climbs a fiber of a radial glial cell.

5
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:26.900
Now imagine its front reaching forward while its nucleus stays behind.

6
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.140
What pulls the nucleus?

7
00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:35.533
Radial glia keep a long fiber running from the ventricle to the outer surface.

8
00:00:36.413 --> 00:00:38.893
The wall is strung with parallel fibers.

9
00:00:39.953 --> 00:00:43.673
A neuron born beside the ventricle can climb such a fiber.

10
00:00:44.653 --> 00:00:50.073
In culture, mouse neurons from the cerebellum were filmed moving along glial fibers.

11
00:00:50.773 --> 00:00:52.793
They moved in a stop-and-go rhythm.

12
00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:57.993
The tip of the cell extended and retracted out of step with the cell body.

13
00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:03.627
The forward movement of the nucleus is called nucleokinesis.

14
00:01:04.087 --> 00:01:06.347
It runs as a two stroke cycle.

15
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:10.067
In stroke one, the leading process extends.

16
00:01:10.887 --> 00:01:13.447
A swelling forms in it, ahead of the nucleus.

17
00:01:14.187 --> 00:01:17.667
The centrosome and the Golgi move forward into the swelling.

18
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:23.350
In stroke two, the nucleus is pulled forward into the swelling.

19
00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.010
The trailing part of the cell follows.

20
00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:28.210
Then the cycle repeats.

21
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:30.370
Think of an inchworm.

22
00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.050
The front reaches and grips.

23
00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:35.270
Then the back catches up.

24
00:01:36.083 --> 00:01:37.683
What does the pulling?

25
00:01:38.723 --> 00:01:44.363
In newborn rat neurons fixed mid-cycle, microtubules wrapped around the nucleus

26
00:01:44.363 --> 00:01:48.043
like a cage. They ran forward toward the centrosome.

27
00:01:48.783 --> 00:01:50.383
That cage is the track.

28
00:01:51.463 --> 00:01:55.403
Dynein is a motor that walks toward the minus ends of microtubules.

29
00:01:56.143 --> 00:01:58.063
Those ends sit at the centrosome.

30
00:01:59.023 --> 00:02:03.643
Anchored around the nucleus, dynein is thought to draw the nucleus toward the

31
00:02:03.643 --> 00:02:07.203
centrosome. Myosin II adds a second force.

32
00:02:08.083 --> 00:02:09.663
Where it acts is still argued.

33
00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:13.927
Normally the nucleus follows the front.

34
00:02:14.947 --> 00:02:16.147
Now break the pull.

35
00:02:16.987 --> 00:02:22.507
Knock down LIS1 in embryonic rat cortex, and the cell body stops moving.

36
00:02:23.507 --> 00:02:25.907
LIS1 regulates dynein.

37
00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:31.827
Losing one copy is enough to cause lissencephaly, a cortex with reduced folding.

38
00:02:32.987 --> 00:02:36.387
Now block myosin II in newborn rat neurons.

39
00:02:37.187 --> 00:02:40.927
The leading process can keep growing while the cell body stops.

40
00:02:41.607 --> 00:02:43.027
The front reaches first.

41
00:02:43.587 --> 00:02:45.427
The nucleus is pulled after it.
